\section{Make Optional Fields Consume Exactly Their Bytes}

A decoder's field cursor is as important as its decoded
operands. For the worked long-mode load, isolate the routine
that runs after reading its legacy opcode and REX prefix.
Handle only the memory form whose ModR/M selects a SIB
and an eight-bit displacement. The caller supplies the start
of this instruction and a cursor at its ModR/M position.
Return the resulting address form and next cursor on success.
Distinguish truncated, overlong, and unsupported outcomes on
failure; no unsuccessful call advances the caller's cursor.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Byte span $B$, instruction start $s$, field cursor $p$
\Let{q}{p}
\To{Each take checks both the span and the instruction limit.}
\Let{(m,q)}{\operatorname{take}(B,s,q,1)}
\If{$m$ does not select the supported memory form}
  \State \Return unsupported
\EndIf
\Let{(b,q)}{\operatorname{take}(B,s,q,1)}
\Let{(d,q)}{\operatorname{take}(B,s,q,1)}
\To{Sign extension interprets the field, not its byte count.}
\Let{A}{\operatorname{addressFromSIB}(b,\operatorname{sign8}(d))}
\State \Return address $A$ and next cursor $q$
\end{sudocode}

For \texttt{4F 8B 4C A5 F0}, with start $0$ and
field cursor $2$, the three takes read \texttt{4C},
\texttt{A5}, and \texttt{F0}. The next cursor is $5$
and the displacement is $-16$. With the same bytes
followed by \texttt{90}, the cursor must still be $5$;
that extra byte belongs to the next instruction.
Remove \texttt{F0}, keeping only four bytes total.
The last take returns truncated, and the caller still
owns the old cursor. This tests byte consumption,
independent of whether the printed register names are correct.

The take helper propagates truncation when the requested
bytes exceed the span, and reports an overlong form if they
would go beyond byte fifteen of this instruction. Its
invariant is that the local cursor measures the consumed
prefix from the original start. The SIB interpretation must
receive the earlier REX bits and any address-size information;
these are implicit in the restricted routine. A full decoder
needs separate cases for other modes, displacement sizes, and
forms that do not contain a SIB.

\section{Test Bytes and Meanings Separately}

Some semantically equivalent instructions have different encodings. Even within a single mnemonic a canonical assembler may shorten a displacement or choose a different form than the one it decoded. Thus \emph{decode after encode} should preserve the typed instruction, up to documented aliases, and consume exactly the bytes emitted; \emph{encode after decode} need not preserve bytes unless the intermediate representation retains the selected form and enough raw details. For the worked load, construct a version with a four-byte sign-extended displacement and compare the decoded address expressions while expecting different lengths. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/39-semantic-fingerprints-and-metamorphic-compiler-testing.md}{Semantic Fingerprints and Metamorphic Compiler Testing} applies tests based on these kinds of relations more broadly.

Test malformed inputs too: a decoder must not consume beyond its supplied buffer if a prefix or addressing byte is truncated, and an architectural x86 instruction cannot exceed 15 bytes. Finally, decoding from an arbitrary offset in a section is not proof that it was a valid instruction boundary. Keep instruction boundary evidence and data regions distinct when reading object or linked code.
